    "body": "Source: ENGLISH SE PART 7.pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nComprehensive & Ultimate LET Reviewer (2025-2026)\nEduPro PH Reviewer\nSECONDARY EDUCATION - ENGLISH PART 7 2025/2026\nMake mine Affections thy Swift Flyers neat And\n1. What is the tone of the following lines from\nmake my Soul thy holy Spool to be.\nShakespeare’s Hamlet?\nMy Conversation make to be thy Reel\nWhat a piece of work is a man! How noble in reason!\nAnd reel the yarn thereon spun of thy Wheel.\nHow infinite in faculties! In form and moving, how\nA. irony of statement\nexpress and admirable!\nB. pathetic fallacy\nA. amazement\nC. a literary conceit\nB. mockery\nD. a paradoxical line\nC. veneration\nD. sadness\n7. What does the persona in “Huswifery” ask God to\ndo?\n2. The following lines from Robert Browning’s\nA. Complete him as a human being\nMy Last Duchess exemplify what poetic strategy?\nB. Bless him with food and clothing\nThat's my last Duchess painted on the wall, Looking\nC. Mold him into what God wants him to be\nas if she were alive. I call\nD. Clothe him with the finest silk from God\nThat piece a wonder, now: Frà Pandolf's hands\nWorked busily a day, and there she stands.\n8. Which two sound devices did Alexander\nWill't please you sit and look at her?\nPope use in the following lines?\nA. Aside\nSoft is the strain when Zephyr gently blows,\nB. Dialogue\nAnd the smooth stream in smoother numbers\nC. Monologue\nflows; but when loud surges lash the sounding\nD. Soliloquy\nshore,\nThe hoarse, rough verse should like the torrent\n3. From what perspective is the following\nroar:\nstory told?\nA. Assonance and consonance\n\"I could picture it. I have a rotten habit of picturing\nB. Alliteration and onomatopoeia\nthe bedroom scenes of my friends. We went out to\nC. Consonance and cacophony\nthe Cafe Napolitain to have an aperitif and watch\nD. Onomatopoeia and assonance\nthe evening crowd on the Boulevard.\"\nfrom The Sun Also Rises by Ernest Hemingway.\n9. What figure of speech is exemplified below?\nA. First person\n“The wind stood up and gave a shout. He whistled\nB. Second person\non his two fingers.”\nC. Third person omniscient\nA. Allusion\nD. Third person limited\nB. Metaphor\nC. Onomatopoeia\n4. What type of irony does Shakespeare use in\nD. Personification\nAnthony’s speech?\nWhen that the poor have cried, Caesar hath wept:\n10. What type of sonnet is exemplified in the\nAmbition should be made of sterner stuff:\nfollowing lines?\nYet Brutus says he was ambitious; And Brutus is an\nWhen I consider how my light is spent\nhonourable man.\nEre half my days, in this dark world and wide, And\nA. dramatic irony\nthat one talent which is death to hide\nC. causal irony\nLodged with me useless, though my soul more bent\nB. irony of situation\nA. Elizabethan\nD. verbal irony\nB. English\nC. Petrarchan\n5. What do the following lines from William\nD. Spenserian\nBlake exhort?\nTo see a World in a Grain of Sand And a Heaven in a\n11. Which statement best summarizes the Holy\nWild Flower,\nSonnet X by John Donne?\nHold Infinity in the palm of your hand And Eternity\nAnd poppy or charms can make us sleep as well\nin an hour.\nAnd better than thy stroke; why swell'st thou then?\nA. to appreciate even the smallest of things\nOne short sleep past, we wake eternally,\nB. to be extremely imaginative and creative\nAnd death shall be no more; death, thou shalt die.\nC. to believe in fantasy like a child\nA. Death shall cease in the after life.\nD. to be strong and faithful to God\nB. Death comes through poppy or charms.\nC. Death takes so many forms and ways.\n6. What poetic device is exemplified in the\nD. Death should not be proud since it is not\nfollowing lines from Edward Taylor’s “Huswifery”?\nmighty.\nMake me, O Lord, thy Spinning Wheel complete,\nThy Holy Word my Distaff make for me.\n12. What does the word “swell’st” in the Holy\n\nComprehensive & Ultimate LET Reviewer (2025-2026)\nEduPro PH Reviewer\nSonnet X mean?\nA. boast\nB. shrink\nC. grow\nD. swear\n13. Which statement about love is true based\non Shakespeare’s Sonnet 116?\nLove's not Time's fool, though rosy lips and cheeks\nWithin his bending sickle's compass come: Love\nalters not with his brief hours and weeks, But bears\nit out even to the edge of doom.\nA. Love dissipates when lovers live apart.\nB. Love adapts to changing circumstances.\nC. Love never wanes even in old age.\nD. Love grows even to the edge of doom.\n14. In “To the Virgins to Make Much of Time,”\nwhat is the persona’s main message?\nA. Be wise in marriage to make life more\nworthwhile.\nB. Marry now, or you may never have another\nchance.\nC. Gather the rosebuds now, before the roses\nbloom.\nD. Choose only lovers who, like roses, are of\nthe highest order.\n15. Which word best describes the speaker in\n“To Lucasta, on Going to the Wars”?\nTell me not, Sweet, I am unkind, That from the\nnunnery\nOf thy chaste breast and quiet mind To war and\narms I fly.\nA. cold-hearted\nB. sweet-tongued\nC. honorable\nD. modest\n16. To what sensory perception do the\nfollowing lines from James Joyce’s Araby appeal?\n“…we ran…to the dark dripping gardens to the back\ndoors of the dark dripping gardens where odors\narose from the ashpits, to the dark odorous stables\nwhere a coachman smoothed and combed the horse\nor shook music from the buckled harness.”\nA. auditory\nB. olfactory\nC. gustatory\nD. tactile\n17. What does the lamb in “The Lamb”\nsymbolize?\nLittle Lamb, who made thee? Dost thou know who\nmade thee? Gave thee life, and bid thee feed,\nBy the stream and o'er the mead;\nA. Faith and loyalty\nB. Weakness and hopelessness\nC. Innocence and purity\nD. Helplessness and dependence",
