    "body": "Source: LET English Grammar Test (9000+ Questions).pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\n18. I think your FAT cat is ill.\nA) thin\nB) stout\nD) thick\nE) small\n\n36. His best KNOWN paintings give a light to today’s Europe.\nA) well-known\nB) famous\nC) unknown\nD) bad\nE) worst\n\nBook 2 Part A\n\nC) big\n\n236\n\nC) short\n\nAntonyms\n\n37. We must never FORGET our heroes.\nA) recognize\nB) recite\nD) forgive\nE) thank\n\nC) remember\n\n38. You are a GOOD swimmer.\nA) hot\nB) bad\nD) normal\nE) not well\n\nC) low\n\n57. We heard a LOT OF interesting things over the radio yesterday\nevening.\nA) little\nB) many\nC) tremendous\nD) few\nE) a great deal\n58. I’ll BE IN tomorrow morning.\nA) stay\nB) be out\nD) come\nE) give\n\n39. I stood there admiring that BEAUTIFUL picture.\nA) bad\nB) nice\nC) ugly\nD) terrible\nE) plain\n40. You can tell him the TRUTH. He will help you.\nA) a form\nB) a tale\nD) a lie\nE) a fable\n\nC) a secret\n\n41. Adriano is a TALL man.\nA) big\nB) little\nD) short\nE) large\n\nC) small\n\nC) be over\n\n59. When the Browns paint their living-room and the paint is DRY they\nwill hang a modern picture on the wall.\nA) cold\nB) white\nC) wet\nD) blue\nE) clean\n60. Though the watch was very EXPENSIVE, he decided to buy it for\nher.\nA) dear\nB) calm\nC) cheap\nD) quiet\nE) rich\n\n42. It’s much QUIETER there than here - very beautiful, but no tourists.\nA) more peaceful\nB) noisier\nC) more essential\nD) more uncertain\nE) easier\n\n61. The Thames is a short river but it is WIDE.\nA) long\nB) shallow\nD) nice\nE) big\n\nC) narrow\n\n43. They are good boys, don’t LAUGH at them!\nA) smile\nB) shout\nD) look\nE) speak\n\n62. He GAVE his friend a book last week.\nA) brought\nB) took\nD) had\nE) sold\n\nC) bought\n\nC) cry\n\n63. John’s parents want him to study law and become President one\nday. They have HIGH hopes on him.\nA) tall\nB) respectful\nC) wise\nD) unpleasant\nE) small\n\n44. I don’t think he will work hard. He is very LAZY.\nA) absent-minded\nB) good looking\nC) good-natured\nD) delighted\nE) hard-working\n45. The mountains in Great Britain are not very HIGH.\nA) low\nB) long\nC) short\nD) tall\nE) big\n\n64. The MORE you read, the more you know.\nA) much\nB) many\nD) least\nE) less\n\nC) little\n\n46. He worked MUCH and got a good mark at his\nA) a lot\nB) many\nD) little\nE) a few\n\nC) few\n\n47. He thinks his son has a GOOD future.\nA) nice\nB) fine\nD) bad\nE) wrong\n\n65. BEFORE death he decided to leave his money to a hospital.\nA) earlier\nB) after\nC) above\nD) below\nE) over\n\nC) kind\n\nC) weak\n\n48. You’ve got WET through.\nA) warm\nB) dry\nD) sweet\nE) cold\n\n66. Nina was a small girl and not at all STRONG.\nA) seldom\nB) forceful\nD) frozen\nE) clever\n\nC) hot\n\nC) easily\n\n49. Did you come by SEA?\nA) land\nB) bed\nD) shop\nE) tree\n\n67. She runs FAST.\nA) quickly\nD) badly\n\nC) desk\n\n50. Don’t speak so FAST!\nA) loudly\nD) proudly\n\nC) well\n\nB) slowly\nE) quickly\n\n51. I’m LEAVING FOR Rio tomorrow and I’ll be back in a week’s time.\nA) going to\nB) coming from\nC) living in\nD) reaching\nE) approaching\n52. My friend Cyril isn’t TALL enough to dance with Natalie.\nA) good\nB) nice\nC) happy\nD) long\nE) short\n\nB) slowly\nE) well\n\n68. Tom was a TALL boy of 16.\nA) low\nB) short\nD) little\nE) small\n\nC) long\n\n69. I must change my jeans. They are really DIRTY.\nA) clear\nB) short\nD) clean\nE) yellow\n\nC) bright\n\n70. It’s EASY to understand this text.\nA) hard\nB) difficult\nD) longest\nE) clear\n\nC) simple\n\n53. When I WOKE UP it was dark in the room.\nA) got up\nB) awoke\nD) stayed\nE) left\n\nC) fell asleep\n\n71. David was POOR and had TO FIND his aunt.\nA) rich / to lose\nB) tall / to win\nC) short / to gather\nD) clever / to hide\nE) rich / to look for\n\n54. I’m glad the interest rate is not very HIGH.\nA) short\nB) tall\nD) low\nE) huge\n\nC) cried\n\nC) long\n\n72. He SAT thinking near the window.\nA) stood\nB) saw\nD) flew\nE) died\n\n55. We are in a hurry. PUSH the car to one side.\nA) bring\nB) move\nD) park\nE) break\n\nC) tired\n\nC) pull\n\n73. I have been UNHAPPY since my mother died.\nA) happy\nB) busy\nD) excited\nE) ill\n74. - How are you?\n- I am WELL, thank you.\nA) ill\nB) greedy\nD) O.K.\nE) ready\n\n56. The old man had MORE money than sixty thousand pounds.\nA) much\nB) larger\nC) over\nD) fewer\nE) less\n\nBook 2 Part A\n\n237\n\nC) fine\n\nAntonyms\n\n75. This box is very light. The man can EASILY lift it.\nA) hardly\nB) hurriedly\nC) slowly\nD) earnestly\nE) badly\n76. My watch is RIGHT.\nA) wrong\nD) slow\n\nB) left\nE) fast\n\n99. Cautious\nA) fussy\n\nB) reckless\n\nC) nervous\n\nD) careful\n\n100. Clever\nA) Stupid\n\nB) chubby\n\nC) kind\n\nD) smart\n\n101. Dangerous\nA) stormy\n\nB) risky\n\nC) safe\n\nD) hazardous\n\n102. Increase\nA) carry off\n\nB) go up\n\nC) decrease\n\nD) rise\n\n103. Begin\nA) continue\n\nB) ban\n\nC) urge\n\nD) stop\n\n104. Narrow\nA) thin\n\nB) tight\n\nC) wide\n\nD) sharp\n\n105. Great\nA) huge\n\nB) big\n\nC) small\n\nD) heavy\n\n106. Outside\nA) above\n\nB) on\n\nC) inside\n\nD) under\n\n107. Ill\nA) sick\n\nB) painful\n\nC) poor\n\nD) healthy\n\nC) heavy\n\n77. It happened long after THE NIGHT that I fell down into a pit.\nA) dark\nB) evening\nC) day\nD) dawn\nE) moonlight\n78. There’s too MUCH crime and violence in the streets of cities\nnowadays.\nA) sad\nB) few\nC) glad\nD) little\nE) many\n79. My RIGHT hand is stronger than yours.\nA) left\nB) wrong\nD) pretty\nE) crushed\n\nC) dirty\n\n80. Scotland is the land of mountains, NARROW valleys and plains.\nA) wide\nB) long\nC) fat\nD) thick\nE) large\nFind the antonym of the following words:\n81. Leave\nA) arrive\n\nB) desert\n\nC) separate\n\nD) abandon\n\n108. Noisy\nA) muddy\n\nB) misty\n\nC) moldy\n\nD) silent\n\n82. Hide\nA) show\n\nB) cover\n\nC) spend\n\nD) save\n\n109. Finish\nA) conclude\n\nB) end\n\nC) terminate\n\nD) begin\n\n83. High\nA) low\n\nB) tall\n\nC) important\n\nD) powerful\n\n110. Light\nA) rainy\n\nB) misty\n\nC) dark\n\nD) bright\n\n84. Brave\nA) heroic\n\nB) bold\n\nC) keen\n\nD) cowardly\n\n111. Hate\nA) praise\n\nB) commend\n\nC) love\n\nD) detest\n\n85. Behind\nA) near\n\nB) far\n\nC) in front of\n\nD) on\n\n112. Cheap\nA) expensive\n\nB) inexpensive C) free\n\nD) low-cost\n\n86. Pull\nA) divide\n\nB) hit\n\nC) kick\n\nD) push\n\n113. Finish\nA) terminate\n\nB) conclude\n\nC) end\n\nD) begin\n\n87. Horizontal\nA) flat\n\nB) level\n\nC) vertical\n\nD) even\n\n114. Fresh\nA) stale\n\nB) new\n\nC) clean\n\nD) pure\n\n88. Absent\nA) present\n\nB) off\n\nC) away\n\nD) missing\n\n115. Find\nA) lose\n\nB) seek\n\nC) locate\n\nD) look\n\n116. Pull\nA) push\n\nB) kick\n\nC) hit\n\nD) divide\n\n117. Cold\nA) misty\n\nB) icy\n\nC) hot\n\nD) warm\n\n118. Begin\nA) ban\n\nB) urge\n\nC) continue\n\nD) stop\n\n119. Cautious\nA) reckless\n\nB) fussy\n\nC) nervous\n\nD) careful\n\n120. Early\nA) now\n\nB) quickly\n\nC) before\n\nD) late\n\n121. Child\nA) girl\n\nB) infant\n\nC) boy\n\nD) adult\n\n122. Minority\nA) majority\n\nB) population\n\nC) mankind\n\nD) people\n\n123. Better\nA) clear\n\nB) calm\n\nC) worse\n\nD) well\n\n124. Enemy\nA) rival\n\nB) opponent\n\nC) friend\n\nD) client",
