    "body": "Source: LET English Grammar Test (9000+ Questions).pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\n36. Don’t try to BUTTER me. This trick of yours won’t work with me.\nA) spread\nB) flatter\nC) press\nD) ban\nE) frighten\n37. The company asked for ADDITIONAL information.\nA) emphatic\nB) careful\nC) certain\nD) further\nE) unusual\n38. The traffic rules must be OBSERVED by everybody.\nA) seen\nB) heard\nC) followed\nD) taken\nE) learnt\n\nC) beautiful\n\n55. He carried a BUNCH of flowers in his hands.\nA) bouquet\nB) bundle\nD) bucket\nE) flock\n\nC) packet\n\n56. Tom is an INDUSTRIOUS boy.\nA) hard working\nB) gracious\nD) huge\nE) lazy\n\nC) graceful\n\n57. Oh, my dear! I’ll be back BEFORE YOU SAY JOHN ROBINSON.\nA) in an hour\nB) hardly\nC) very soon\nD) at sunset\nE) in some time\n\n39. Children need some RELAXATION after all those hard exams.\nA) vacation\nB) holidays\nC) time\nD) period\nE) rest\n40. -Who’s that man you spoke to just now?\n-I don’t know, he is completely UNFAMILIAR to me.\nA) foreign\nB) strange\nC) unknown\nD) new\nE) for\n41. Grey’s going TO GET a splendid job.\nA) to go\nB) to put\nD) to receive\nE) to win\n\n54. A SMOOTHFACED man of forty faced me.\nA) graceful\nB) shaven\nD) famous\nE) handsome\n\n58. What DIARY PRODUCTS do you like to eat?\nA) creamery\nB) meals\nD) first course\nE) desserts\n\nC) animal\n\n59. Puppies lived in a DOG-HOUSE.\nA) kennel\nB) barn\nD) box\nE) garage\n\nC) cave\n\nC) to obtain\n60. The tickets to the theatre will be booked IN ADVANCE.\nA) later\nB) soon\nC) the next day\nD) earlier\nE) beforehand\n\n42. He is a person who understands his OBLIGATIONS and attends\nto them.\nA) restrictions\nB) annoyances\nC) observations\nD) hardship\nE) duties\n\n61. I have no idea where the relatives on my father’s side live, LET\nALONE visiting them.\nA) not a single\nB) leave alone\nC) only one of\nD) not speaking of\nE) lonely one\n\n43. Mount Cook, THE CROWN of the New Zealand Southern Alps,\nrises to 3756 meters above the surface.\nA) summit\nB) foot\nC) beauty\nD) rock\nE) earth\n\n62. Vasco da Gama was a Portuguese EXPLORER born around 1460.\nA) traveler\nB) scientist\nC) king\nD) ruler\nE) conqueror\n\n44. He was PUT TO DEATH 2 days go.\nA) released\nB) left\nD) found\nE) disappeared\n\nC) executed\n\n63. The Vikings liked to make up long tales about their BRAVE deeds.\nA) timid\nB) fearless\nC) past\nD) strong\nE) stupid\n\n45. What EXCUSE have you got this time?\nA) matter\nB) problem\nD) reason\nE) explanation\n\nC) factor\n\n64. The daily performance was killingly DULL.\nA) unforgettable\nB) dutiful\nD) boring\nE) bright\n\n46. The professor’s INTRODUCTORY remarks concerned the\ndevelopment of culture in that region.\nA) preliminary\nB) final\nC) next\nD) supplementary\nE) useful\n47. The captain of the ship LEFT his town.\nA) abandoned\nB) sought\nD) looked for\nE) left for\n\n65. Doctor Manson CURED a lot of miners that’s why he won great\npopularity and respect with them.\nA) treated\nB) recovered\nC) gave\nD) took\nE) rescued\n66. Australian aborigines are dark skinned people whose\nDESCENDANTS came to the continent from Asia about 25,000\nyears ago.\nA) predecessors\nB) relatives\nC) offsprings\nD) colonists\nE) supporters\n\nC) visited\n\n48. When I opened the box at home I FOUND that the shoes were not\nmine.\nA) talked\nB) sent\nC) recognized\nD) discovered\nE) called\n\n67. I LIKE collecting stamps.\nA) am ill\nB) am good at\nD) am afraid of\nE) am proud of\n\n49. I offered him a cup of coffee, which he REFUSED politely.\nA) rejected\nB) took\nC) gave\nD) finished\nE) obtained\n50. It was a lucky CHANCE that he could do it.\nA) business\nB) opportunity\nD) manager\nE) pension\n\nC) dynamic\n\nC) am fond of\n\n68. I am a BIG FISH IN A SMALL COMPANY.\nA) to be important in a big company\nB) to be respected everywhere\nC) to be important in a small company\nD) to be estimated everywhere\nE) not to be important in a small company\n\nC) matter\n\n51. I want a set of books for someone who is KEEN ON reading.\nA) severe on\nB) quick at\nC) fond of\nD) sharp at\nE) afraid of\n\n69. Nobody believed his VOW because it wasn’t for the first time.\nA) belief\nB) idea\nC) view\nD) oath\nE) opinion\n\n52. In Sydney, William Westwood was turned over to a cruel settler\nas AN UNPAID laborer.\nA) a paid\nB) a free\nC) an illegal\nD) an unnoticed\nE) a permanent\n\n70. The first colonists from England made new homes and began TO\nWIDEN industrial towns.\nA) establish\nB) waste\nC) broaden\nD) burden\nE) steal\n\nBook 2 Part B\n\n252\n\nSynonyms\n\n71. He STUDIED the document for a long time.\nA) read carefully\nB) taught\nD) examined carefully E) looked through\n\nC) looked at\n\n72. Did you manage to stop the FIGHT between those two boys?\nA) show\nB) stage\nC) picture\nD) talk\nE) struggle\n73. The woman adds water to UNITE the flour and the milk.\nA) connect\nB) make\nC) join\nD) complex\nE) combine\n74. I can call back the faint ODORS of the wild flowers.\nA) orders\nB) ado\nC) fits\nD) harmony\nE) fragrances\n75. If the shops were not so CROWDED, the clerks would not be so\ntireD)\nA) cold\nB) full\nC) old\nD) fresh\nE) fast\n76. Her interest in people and other animals was warm, personal\nand FRIENDLY.\nA) cordial\nB) antagonistic\nC) harmful\nD) cool\nE) hostile\n77. A dolphin asks for HELP.\nA) assistance\nB) assistant\nD) provision\nE) sponsor\n\nC) support\n\n78. Edison’s idea was TO CHECK the mistakes of his son.\nA) to rise\nB) to develop\nC) to verify\nD) to close\nE) to renew\nFind the synonym of the following words.\n\n93. Unattended\nA) sluggish\n\nB) heedless\n\nC) alone\n\nD) empty\n\n94. Group\nA) parcel\n\nB) party\n\nC) package\n\nD) present\n\n95. Task\nA) lead\n\nB) donation\n\nC) summit\n\nD) mission\n\n96. Peak\nA) bottom\n\nB) summit\n\nC) rear\n\nD) front\n\n97. Bump\nA) jolt\n\nB) piece\n\nC) lump\n\nD) handle\n\n98. Command\nA) urge\n\nB) force\n\nC) order\n\nD) seize\n\n99. Enterprise\nA) hospitality B) immunity\n\nC) imagination D) undertaking\n\n100. Resident\nA) confident\n\nB) hesitant\n\nC) incident\n\nD) inhabitant\n\n101. Identify\nA) bump\n\nB) throw in\n\nC) catch\n\nD) recognize\n\n102. Beg\nA) predict\n\nB) implore\n\nC) guess\n\nD) oblige\n\n103. Spell\nA) native\n\nB) period\n\nC) shade\n\nD) obligation\n\n104. Delight\nA) sadness\n\nB) joy\n\nC) calmness\n\nD) gloom\n\n79. Important\nA) complex\n\nB) secondary\n\nC) detailed\n\nD) significant\n\n105. Genuine\nA) fake\n\nB) counterfeit\n\nC) false\n\nD) authentic\n\n80. Stubborn\nA) shy\n\nB) obstinate\n\nC) pliable\n\nD) yielding\n\n106. Damage\nA) mend\n\nB) fix\n\nC) incite\n\nD) ravage\n\n81. Accustomed\nA) flexible\n\nB) limp\n\nC) stiff\n\nD) used to\n\n107. Use up\nA) consume\n\nB) stack\n\nC) draw up\n\nD) put by\n\n82. Stare\nA) peep\n\nB) pry\n\nC) gaze\n\nD) pause\n\n108. Behavior\nA) business\n\nB) conduct\n\nC) connection\n\nD) container\n\nC) prompt\n\nD) delayed\n\n109. Stationary\nA) stationery\n\nB) portable\n\nC) mobile\n\nD) still\n\n84. Keen\nA) slow\n\nB) enthusiastic C) reluctant\n\nD) apathetic",
