    "body": "Source: LET English Grammar Test (9000+ Questions).pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\n110. Walk over\nA) wash up\n\nB) defeat\n\nC) keep away\n\nD) waver\n\n85. Duty\nA) obligation\n\nB) shade\n\nC) period\n\nD) native\n\n111. Praise\nA) blame\n\nB) commend\n\nC) censure\n\nD) criticize\n\n86. Shorten\nA) shrink\n\nB) thrust\n\nC) fling\n\nD) shove\n\n112. Mystery\nA) coherent\n\nB) enigma\n\nC) curious\n\nD) reverse\n\n87. Firm\nA) soft\n\nB) solid\n\nC) uneven\n\nD) loose\n\n113. Stop\nA) cease\n\nB) eliminate\n\nC) commence\n\nD) commend\n\n88. Rubbish\nA) scrap\n\nB) crump\n\nC) stone\n\nD) litter\n\n114. Respect\nA) esteem\n\nB) belief\n\nC) scorn\n\nD) contempt\n\n89. Peril\nA) danger\n\nB) secure\n\nC) hole\n\nD) safe\n\n115. Splendid\nA) terrible\n\nB) dreadful\n\nC) awful\n\nD) marvelous\n\n90. Flush\nA) bruise\n\nB) blush\n\nC) rush\n\nD) brush\n\n116. Exhausted\nA) anxious\n\nB) worn-out\n\nC) cheerful\n\nD) vigorous\n\n91. Bring round\nA) persuade\n\nB) bring back\n\nC) bring on\n\nD) bring down\n\n117. Sway\nA) river\n\nB) shudder\n\nC) ruin\n\n118. Widespread\nA) Infrequent\n\nB) scarce\n\nC) prevalent\n\n83. Overdue\nA) impending B) punctual\n\n92. Bring forward\nA) bring down B) bring up\n\nBook 2 Part B\n\nC) bring round D) suggest\n\n253\n\nD) swing\n\nD) rare\n\nSynonyms\n\n119. Defect\nA) advantage\n\nB) clash\n\nC) decay\n\nD) drawback\n\n145. Ban\nA) produce\n\nB) prohibit\n\nC) create\n\nD) happen\n\n120. Force\nA) oblige\n\nB) eliminate\n\nC) halt\n\nD) liberate\n\n146. Raw\nA) rotten\n\nB) tough\n\nC) moldy\n\nD) uncooked\n\n121. Talkative\nA) Know-all\n\nB) scrupulous\n\nC) chatty\n\nD) fastidious\n\n147. Lucrative\nA) crucial\n\nB) fragile\n\nC) profitable\n\nD) decisive\n\n122. Precious\nA) Enormous\n\nB) vigorous\n\nC) gorgeous\n\nD) valuable\n\n148. Discover\nA) carry out\n\nB) sort out\n\nC) find out\n\nD) bring out\n\n123. Profession\nA) celebration B) restoration C) attention\n\nD) occupation\n\n149. Simply\nA) merely\n\nB) actually\n\nC) usually\n\nD) frequently\n\n124. Contrary\nA) customary\n\nC) common\n\nD) opposite\n\n150. Classify\nA) tie up\n\nB) sort out\n\nC) stir up\n\nD) tear up\n\n125. Unpredictable\nA) constant\nB) steady\n\nC) dense\n\nD) volatile\n\n151. Devious\nA) scornful\n\nB) liberal\n\nC) honest\n\nD) crooked\n\n126. Row\nA) contest\n\nB) argument\n\nC) chat\n\nD) quiz\n\n152. Stress\nA) omit\n\nB) emphasize\n\nC) leave out\n\nD) ignore\n\n127. Hug\nA) embrace\n\nB) push\n\nC) stab\n\nD) poke\n\n153. Contaminate\nA) renovate\n\nB) mend\n\nC) purify\n\nD) pollute\n\n128. Ban\nA) prohibit\n\nB) produce\n\nC) create\n\nD) happen\n\n154. Timetable\nA) scheme\n\nB) plot\n\nC) schedule\n\nD) minutes\n\n129. Sign\nA) truth\n\nB) trace\n\nC) truce\n\nD) trunk\n\n155. Slowly\nA) briefly\n\nB) gradually\n\nC) deeply\n\nD) mainly\n\n130. Brag\nA) enrage\n\nB) boast\n\nC) madden\n\nD) infuriate\n\n156. Harm\nA) Peril\n\nB) trouble\n\nC) damage\n\nD) rush\n\n131. Disagreement\nA) poise\nB) conflict\n\nC) harmony\n\nD) stamina\n\n157. Incidentally\nA) far away\n\nB) in any case C) by the way\n\nD) in my opinion\n\n132. Enormous\nA) tidy\n\nB) shoddy\n\nC) mini\n\nD) huge\n\n158. Summit\nA) rear\n\nB) bottom\n\nC) peak\n\nD) front\n\n133. Hang on\nA) rely on\n\nB) hold on\n\nC) keep on\n\nD) count on\n\n159. Skin\nA) peel\n\nB) pip\n\nC) seed\n\nD) stone\n\n134. Take away\nA) spread\n\nB) distribute\n\nC) hand over\n\nD) remove\n\n160. Walk out\nA) leave\n\nB) lay out\n\nC) work out\n\nD) burst out\n\n135. Outcome\nA) arrival\n\nB) exit\n\nC) result\n\nD) commence\n\n161. Glimmer\nA) darken\n\nB) shimmer\n\nC) slit\n\nD) shelter\n\n136. Sufficient\nA) fake\n\nB) unreal\n\nC) adequate\n\nD) rare\n\n162. Goods\nA) legacy\n\nB) heritage\n\nC) possessions D) patrimony\n\n137. Maintenance\nA) heritage\n\nB) racket\n\nC) alimony\n\nD) extortion\n\nB) conflict\n\n138. Material\nA) supervision B) substance\n\nC) superstition D) surface\n\n139. Run down\nA) turn over\n\nB) run over\n\nC) find out\n\nD) criticize\n\n140. Fling\nA) let in\n\nB) hold\n\nC) hurl\n\nD) seize\n\n141. Temporary\nA) provisional B) permanent\n\nC) constant\n\nD) enduring\n\n142. Go off\nA) spoil\n\nB) break\n\nC) clear up\n\nD) rest\n\n143. Remedy\nA) gift\n\nB) argument\n\nC) reward\n\nD) cure\n\n144. Seldom\nA) rarely\n\nB) frequently\n\nC) regularly\n\nD) often\n\nBook 2 Part B\n\n254\n\nSynonyms\n\nANTONYMS\n\nTEST MASTER\n21. The day before yesterday I WENT TO SEE my sick grandmother.\nA) came in\nB) visited\nC) left\nD) called on\nE) invited\n\nFind the antonym of the following words written in capitals:\n1.\n\n2.\n\nHe was an HONEST man.\nA) liar\nB) good\nD) truthful\nE) wonderful\nThe climate of Great Britain is MILD.\nA) difficult\nB) strong\nD) bad\nE) good\n\nC) bad\n\nC) severe\n\n3.\n\nShe was afraid to walk FARTHER as she knew her life was in danger.\nA) mother\nB) out near\nC) near\nD) nearer\nE) away\n\n4.\n\nIf you know one FOREIGN language it will be easier for you to\nlearn the second one.\nA) local\nB) modern\nC) popular\nD) old\nE) native\n\n5.\n\n6.\n\n7.\n\n8.\n\n9.\n\n22. Her character is very MILD.\nA) gentle\nB) angry\nD) rude\nE) bad\n\nC) difficult\n\n23. The British seem to like their weather as it is ISLAND weather.\nA) isolated\nB) light\nC) mild\nD) difficult\nE) continental\n24. I think it is a LEGAL party as it has been functioning for a long time.\nA) lawful\nB) possible\nC) illegal\nD) illiterate\nE) important\n25. My mother was a KIND person.\nA) cruel\nB) good-natured\nD) bad\nE) merry\n\nC) nice\n\nTom’s father was a CRUEL man.\nA) honest\nB) kind\nD) serious\nE) light\n\nC) funny\n\nIt seemed to her that he was very DECENT.\nA) dishonest\nB) be tired of\nD) handsome\nE) unhealthy\n\n26. About one million Welshmen still speak NATIVE language.\nA) original\nB) natural\nC) folk\nD) other\nE) foreign\n\nC) fat\n\n27. MODERN factories have sprung up around the city.\nA) small\nB) new\nC) out of date\nD) fresh\nE) bad\n\nI opened the door and saw a DECEASED man.\nA) weak\nB) sick\nC) invisible\nD) new born\nE) old\n\n28. The Welsh ARE FULL OF idealism and good humor.\nA) complete\nB) whole\nC) weak\nD) lack\nE) empty\n\nTom is very LAZY. He doesn’t like to do anything.\nA) modest\nB) modern\nC) clever\nD) energetic\nE) nervous\nMy sister is very SERIOUS.\nA) energetic\nB) polite\nD) intelligent\nE) light-minded\n\n29. When he was nine, he ENTERED the gymnasium and became an\nexcellent student.\nA) finished\nB) got\nC) went away\nD) came\nE) completed\n\nC) kind\n\n30. She likes GETTING letters but dislikes writing them.\nA) receiving\nB) taking\nC) sending\nD) reading\nE) finding\n\n10. Mr. Brown decided TO PROTECT that young man.\nA) to help\nB) to accuse\nC) to care\nD) to shout\nE) to criticize\n11. He lives A LONG WAY FROM his school.\nA) far\nB) near\nD) behind\nE) in front of\n\n31. There are many ANCIENT cities in Turkey.\nA) big\nB) old\nD) modern\nE) beautiful\n\nC) late\n\nC) fashionable\n\n32. There’s a DEEP lake between these two villages.\nA) shallow\nB) charming\nC) good-looking\nD) handsome\nE) unpleasant\n\n12. Such trees grow only in the countries the climate of which is hot\nand DAMP.\nA) cold\nB) cool\nC) dry\nD) sunny\nE) windy",
