    "body": "Source: 4. ENGLISH & PHILIPPINE LITERATURE NOTES.pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nProverbs 17:22\n\"A merry heart does\ngood like medicine.”\n\nEnglish\n(Literature)\nLITERATURE\n\n\n\n\nDerived from the Latin word “litera” meaning\nLETTER.\nIt is a faithful reproduction of man’s various\nexperiences blended into one harmonious\nexpression.\nThis is an expression of man’s loves, griefs,\nthoughts, dreams and aspirations communicated\nin beautiful language.\n\nTYPES OF LITERATURE:\n1. POETRY\na. Lyric - expresses emotions and feelings of the\npoet. This is usually easy to understand and\nshort literatures.\nSonnet\nElegy\nPsalms\nAwit\nFlorante at Laura\nCorrido\nIbong Adarna\nFolksongs\n(Awiting\nbayan)\nOde\n\n14 line poem dealing with\nemotions, feelings or ideas\nPoem for the dead\nSongs praising God and\ncontaining a philosophy of\nlife\nThis is sung and is realistic\n12 syllables per line\nRecited and with elements\nof fantasy\nComposed of 8 syllables\nShort poems intended to be\nsung\nLove, despair, grief, doubt,\njoy, hope and sorrow are\ncommon themes\nPoem of a noble feeling,\nexpressed with dignity on a\ncertain object\n\nBallad\n\nExtended narrative about\nheroic exploits often\nunder\nsupernatural\ncontrol\n\nc. Dramatic Comedy\nComedy\n\nForce\n\nMelodrama\n\nTragedy\n(Ex: Hamlet)\n\nDerived from the Greek word\n“komos”\nwhich\nmeans\nfestivity or revelry.\nIts purpose is for amusement\nor happy ending.\nAn\nexaggerated\ncomedy\nwhich seeks to arouse mirth\nby laughable lines.\nSituations are too ridiculous\nto be true\nArouses\nimmediate\nand\nintense emotion and is usually\nsad but a happy ending is set\nfor the principal character.\nInvolves\nthe\nhero\nwho\nstruggles\nmighty\nagainst\ndynamic forces until he meets\ndeath.\n\n2. PROSE\n\nb. Narrative\nTales\n\nEpic\n Biag ni Lam-Ang\n(Ilokos)\n Ybalon (Bicol)\n Bidasari\n(Mindanao)\n Hudhud at Alim\n(Igorot)\n Beowulf\n(England)\n Iliad\nand\nOdyssey\n(Greece)\n\nStories about supernatural\nbeing\nShort poems adapted for\nsinging, simple plot and\nmetrical structure\n\nLegends\nAnecdotes\n\nShort story\nDR. CARL E. BALITA REVIEW CENTER TEL. NO. 735-4098/7350740\n\nFictions, narratives which are\nusually about origins\nCreation\nof\nthe\nwriter’s\nimagination and the main is to\nbring out lessons to the reader\nA narration involving one or\n-1-\n\nEssay\nBiography\nNovel\n\nPlays\nFables\n\nmore characters in one plot and\none single impression.\nExpresses the viewpoint or\nopinion of the writer about a\nparticular problem or event.\nDeals with the life of a person\nwhich maybe about himself or\nthat of others\nLong narration divided into\nchapters. The events are taken\nfrom true to life stories and\nspans a long period of time\nPresented on stage divided into\nacts and each act has many\nscenes\nStories about animals and\ninanimate things that speak and\nact like people and their purpose\nis to enlighten the minds of\nchildren to events that can mold\ntheir ways and attitudes.\n\nFICTION - a prose imaginative composition which\nmay or may not be based on history or fact.\n\nTypes of Fiction:\n1. Short story - relatively brief prose fictional\ncomposition based on a single main incident\nwhich is designed to produce a single dominant\nimpression\n2. Novel - a prose fictional work of considerable\nlength that deals with a series of complications\ninvolving characters in a particular setting\n3. Drama - a composition in prose or verse\ndesigned for stage performance through mime\nand dialogue\n4. Allegory - a symbolic fictional account\nconveying meanings beyond the literal\n\nElements of Fiction\n\n1. Character\n Moral agents of actions\n Invented personages in fiction\nTYPES\nMAJOR\nMINOR\n1. Protagonist - Central\n1. Foil - opposite traits\ncharacter where the\nof the main\nstory revolves\nprotagonist\n2. Antagonist - Prevents 2. Confidant - serves\nthe protagonist in\nas the friend of the\nsolving the conflict\nprotagonist\n3. Background\ncharacters - not\nclosely related with\nthe protagonist\n\nKINDS\nRound: character was Flat: there was no\nable to undergo change\nchange in the outlook\nand action of the\ncharacter\n2. Setting\n Serves as the background of the story, may it be\nphysical, mental or spiritual.\n Serves as the backdrop and sets the mood of\nthe characters.\n\nTime\nPlace\nAtmosphere\n\nELEMENTS\nSets the duration of\nevents\nLocality of the events\nEmotion or the mood\n\nthe\n\n3. Plot\n The arrangement of incidents, the narrative\nstructure, the organization of a narrative and\nthe logical sequence of actions.\nTYPES\nOrganic\nEpisodic\nThe story sprouted There are two or more\nfrom just one conflict\nsources of conflicts\nORGANIZATION\nChronology\nClimax\nThe\nevents\nare The\nevents\nare\narranged according to organized according to\ntime and space\norder of suspense\n4. Conflict\n Considered as the soul of the plot and it is the\ntension between opposing forces in the story.\n External: conflict is from outside forces\n Internal: conflict resides with the main character\n Types:\na. Physical - Man vs. nature\nb. Social - Man vs. man\nc. Psychological - Man vs. self\nd. Cosmic - Man vs. God\n5. Points of View\n Vantage point where the story is narrated\nFirst person\nSecond person\n\nThird person\n(Unlimited)\nThird person\n\nDR. CARL E. BALITA REVIEW CENTER TEL. NO. 735-4098/7350740\n\nA principal character in the\nstory in the one narrating it\nAn indirect disclosure of the\nnarrating\nself\nfor\ncharacterization\nand\nanalysis\nAKA Omniscient point of\nview where the narrator is\nan all-knowing maker\nAKA Central intelligence\n-2-\n\n(Limited)\n\nCamera Eye\n\nRevolving\nComposite\n\npoint of view wherein the\nauthor chooses a character\nfrom whose consciousness\nthe entire story is told\nPresents the dialogues, and\nthe incidents of a narrative\nlike a mechanical recording\ndevice\nCharacterized by a narrative\nshift from one point of view\nto another\nComprehensive view of the\nevents and incidents in the\nstory through the different\nangles adapted by several\nnarrating characters\n\nWORLD LITERATURES\nIliad\nOdyssey\nThe Republic\nThe Epic of Gilgamesh\nThe Confessions\nThe Aeneid\nAlcestis\nMedea\nHippolytus\nThe Trojan Women\nElectra\nThe Book of Mencius\nRomeo and Juliet\nMuch Ado About Nothing\nTwelfth Knight\nHamlet\nMerchant of Venice\nThe School of Wives\nTartuffe\nThe Would-Be Gentleman\nTristram Shandy\nThe Ancient Mariner\nChristabel\nKublai Khan\nSecond Treatise of\nGovernment\nTom Jones\nConfessions\nThe Social Contact\nEthics\nPolitics\nPoetics\nThe Histories\nMeditations\nThe Ramayana\nThe Mahabharata\nThe Prelude\nDead Souls\n\nHomer\nPlato\nLeqi-Unninni\nSt. Augustine\nVirgil\nEuripides\n\nMencius\nWilliam Shakespeare\n\nMoliere\nLaurence Sterne\nSamuel Coleridge\nJohn Locke\nHenry Fielding\nJean Jacques\nRousseau\nAristotle\nHerodotus\nMarcus Aurelius\nValmiki\nVyasa\nWilliam Wordsworth\nNikolai Gogol",
