    "title": "LET English Major: LET REVIEW UNIVERSITY ENGLISH NOTES PART (2) (Part 2)",
    "body": "Source: 13. LET REVIEW UNIVERSITY ENGLISH NOTES PART  (2).pdf\nSource type: pdf\n\nThe reason I came late was because I overslept..\nThe reason I came late was that I overslept.\nThe reason that he makes poor grades is because of his poor study\nhabits.\nThe reason that (or why) he makes poor grades is his poor study\nhabits.\n\nsays, said. Said is the past tense.\nWrong\n\n:\n\nJohn ran into the room and says, \" There is a stranger outside.\"\n\nRight\n\n:\n\nJohn ran into the room and said, \" There is a stranger outside.\"\n\nseldom ever, seldom or ever are incorrect. Use seldom if ever or hardly ever.\nWrong\n\n:\n\nHe seldom ever plays tennis.\n\nRight\n\n:\n\nHe seldom if ever (or hardly ever) plays tennis.\n\nsit, set. Sit does not take an object; set takes an object. The principal parts of the\nverb sit are: sit (present), sat (past), sat (past participle), and sitting (present\nparticiple). The principal parts of the verb set are: set (present), set (past), set (past\nparticiple), and setting (present participle ).\nThe old man is sitting under the tree. (present participle)\nShe set the chair beside the window. (past tense)\nHe sat down to rest. (past tense)\nThey set a date for the meeting. (past tense)\nSit here. (present tense)\nThe guests are sitting at the table. (present participle)\n\n5.\n\n6.\n\nso that. So that refers to \" purpose.\" Do not omit \" that.\"\nWrong\n\n:\n\nHe came here so he could study English.\n\nRight\n\n:\n\nHe came here so that he could study English.\n\nsuch. Do not use such to mean \" very.\" In a result clause; such should be followed\nby that.\nWrong\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nNew York is such a crowded city.\nNew York is a very crowded city.\n\nRight\n\n:\n\nNew York is such a crowded city that I wouldn't like to live there.\n\n5 | LET REVIEWER UNIVERSITY\n\n7.\n\n8.\n\n9.\n\n10.\n\nsuperior is followed by the preposition to (not than ).\nWrong\n\n:\n\nMy toothpaste is superior than the other brands on the market.\n\nRight\n\n:\n\nMy toothpaste is superior to the other brands on the market.\n\nsure, and try are not followed by and.\nWrong\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nBe sure and study for the test.\nBe sure to study for the test.\n\nWrong\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nTry and get some rest.\nTry to get some rest.\n\nthat. Do not omit that in noun clause.\nWrong\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nWrong\n\n:\n\nRight\n\n:\n\nMy objective to smoking is it is dangerous to health.\nMy objective to smoking is that it is dangerous to health.\nHe assured me he understood my problem and he would try to help\nme.\nHe assured me that he understood my problem and that he would try to\nhelp me.\n\ntheir, there, they’re.\nThey're over there beside the cabinet.\nI am not interested in their motives.\nThere was nothing left to do.\nThere are several reasons for his actions.\nThey're coming later this afternoon.\n\n1.\n\nits, it’s. It's is a contraction of it is. Its is a possessive pronoun.\nIt's essential that we leave on time.\nIts habits have been studied in detail.\n\n2.\n\n3.\n\n4.\n\nkind, sort are singular words which may be modified by that or this.\nWrong\nRight\nRight\n\n:\n:\n:\n\nI like these kind of students.\nI like this kind of student.\nThese kinds of machines are very useful.\n\nWrong\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nThose sort of people amuse me.\nThat sort of person amuses me.\n\nkind of a, sort of a. Omit \"a.\"\nWrong\n\n:\n\nWhat sort of a wife is she?\n\nRight\n\n:\n\nWhat sort of wife is she?\n\nkind of, sort of are sometimes confused with the word \" somewhat.\"\n\n6 | LET REVIEWER UNIVERSITY\n\n5.\n\n6.\n\n7.\n\nWrong\n\n:\n\nThe record was kind of expensive.\n\nRight\n\n:\n\nThe record was somewhat expensive.\n\nlater, latter. Latter refers to the last name of two. Do not use latter if more than two\nare named. Later is the comparative of late.\nWrong\n\n:\n\nJohn, Mary, and Susan entered the room. The latter was carrying a\ntypewriter.\n\nRight\n\n:\n\nJohn, Mary, and Susan entered the room. The last named was carrying\na typewriter.\n\nRight\n\n:\n\nThere are a typewriter and a pencil sharpener on the desk. The latter\nneeds to be repaired.\n\nlay, lie. The verb lay has an object; the verb lie does to have an object. The\nprincipal parts of the verb lay are: lay (present), laid (past), laid (past participle), and\nlaying (present participle). The principal parts of the verb lie are: lie (present), lay\n(past), lain (past participle), and lying (present participle ).\nWrong\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nI always lay down after I eat dinner.\nI always lie down after I eat dinner. (present tense)\n\nWrong\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nHe laid down because he had a headache.\nHe lay down because he had a headache. (past tense)\n\nWrong\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nThe books are laying on the table.\nThe books are lying on the table (present participle)\n\nRight\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nThe teacher laid her books on her desk. (past tense)\nThe boys have lain under the tree for several hours. (past participle)\n\nRight\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nShe has laid her head on the pillow. (past participle)\nHer head lies on the pillow. (present tense)\n\nleave, let are sometimes confused.\nWrong\nRight\nRight\n\n8.\n\nShe will not leave me smoke inside the classroom.\nShe will not let me smoke inside the classroom.\nLeave (or let) me alone.\n\nlend, loan. Lend is a verb; loan is a noun.\nWrong\nRight\nRight\n\n9.\n\n:\n:\n:\n\n:\n:\n:\n\nWould you loan me your pencil.\nWould you lend me your pencil?\nHe went to the bank to take out a loan.\n\nlike, as, as if. Like is a preposition; as, as if (or as though) are conjunctions. As is\nalso a preposition when it means \" in the capacity of.\"\nWrong\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nShe doesn't study like she should.\nShe doesn't study as she should.\n\nWrong\n\n:\n\nShe acts like she doesn't understand.\n\n7 | LET REVIEWER UNIVERSITY\n\n10.\n\nRight\n\n:\n\nShe acts as if (or as though) she doesn't understand.\n\nRight\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nAs a member of the organization, I have a right to vote.\nHe eats like a bird.\n\nlose, loose. Lose is a verb. Loose means \" not tight.\"\nShe will lose weight if she goes on a diet.\nOne of the buttons on my shirt is loose.\nmaybe, may be. Maybe means \" perhaps.\" May be is a verb form.\n\n.\n\nWrong\nRight\nRight\n\n2.\n\n3.\n\n:\n:\n:\n\nMay be the meeting will be cancelled.\nMaybe the meeting will be cancelled.\nThe meeting may be cancelled.\n\nmyself, himself, yourself are reflexive pronouns. Do not use them unless they\nrefer back to the subject.\nWrong\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nNo one attended the meeting besides ourselves.\nNo one attended the meeting besides us.\n\nWrong\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nA man like himself deserves praise.\nA man like him deserves praise.\n\nWrong\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nOnly John and myself witnessed the accident.\nOnly John and I witnessed the accident.\n\nRight\nRight\n\n:\n:\n\nI wrote the composition by myself.\nThey themselves did the research. (or They did the research\nthemselves. )\n\noff of. Omit \" of.\"\nHe fell off the horse.\n\n4.\n\n5.\n\n6.",
